+# Falcon-1024 validator key management and rotation (chain 912701 test net)
+
+## Key file format (implemented: `Falcon1024KeyFile`)
+- Format `falcon1024-enc-v1`: scrypt (N=2^17, r=8, p=1) → AES-256-GCM. Header fields are authenticated as AAD.
+- Password source (one of): `BESU_FALCON1024_KEY_PASSWORD_FILE` (preferred; must be 0600) or `BESU_FALCON1024_KEY_PASSWORD`.
+- `BESU_FALCON1024_REQUIRE_ENCRYPTED=true` refuses plaintext key files.
+- Generation, storage and loading enforce owner-only 0600 permissions. Wrong password, a tampered file, wrong permissions and a wrong format are all rejected (unit tests: `Falcon1024KeyFileTest`).
+- Backup and restore: `falcon-net/tools/key-backup.sh` (encrypted file + checksum; the restore test re-derives the same address).
+- Leak check: `falcon-net/tools/leak-scan.py` scans logs, RPC responses and the explorer/attester output for private key material and password bytes (last run: TOTAL_HITS=0).
+
+## Rotation procedure (executed once on node4: 0x5b2bb078… → 0x9330064546…)
+1. Generate the new key on the validator host (encrypted, 0600). Never copy keys between hosts.
+2. Propose an ADD vote for the new address on more than 50% of validators (`qbft_proposeValidatorVote(addr, true)`). Wait until it is in `qbft_getValidatorsByBlockNumber(latest)`.
+   n=4 becomes n=5, and the Falcon quorum becomes ceil(2·5/3)=4.
+3. Stop the node (never run two processes with the same validator key). Start it with the new key file and password file.
+4. Propose a DROP vote for the old address on more than 50% of the (now 5) validators. Wait until the set is back to 4.
+5. Update the ML-DSA attester registry. The registry binds each ML-DSA key to its Falcon validator address with a `PQ_BINDING` envelope.
+   Then restart the attester and check that the registry matches the head validator set.
+6. Archive the old key (encrypted) until no block that it sealed needs re-verification by anyone. Then destroy it.
+
+Risks:
+- While n=5, one more offline node blocks progress (quorum 4 of 5).
+- Doing step 3 before step 2 has finalized takes the validator offline. Doing step 4 before step 3 has the same effect.
+- Emergency rotation (key compromise): drop the compromised address first only if the remaining set still has a quorum. Otherwise add first.
